  "transcript": "# Resilience of the UK's food supply to disruptions\n\n## Big picture: resilient so far, but risks rising\n> The food system has coped with recent shocks, but climate, energy costs, reduced domestic production and policy uncertainty are increasing risk.\n\nChair: We’re dedicating time this week to look at the UK’s food supply resilience. I’ll start by asking: what are the biggest challenges farmers currently face?\nJim Bligh: Retailers rely on a resilient supply chain. Recent shocks—from covid to Ukraine and Iran—show the system can adapt, but risks are growing: climate, energy and water, cyber-security, rules and geopolitics.\nBalwinder Dhoot: The sector has been resilient, but domestic production is slowly eroding. Trade and factory relocation mean we could rely on imports more—manageable now, but risky if the trend continues.\nHarry Metcalfe: Farming varies across the country. In my arable area we’ve had four poor harvests since 2020 and 2026 was the smallest since records began. Climate variability is the immediate problem for growers.\n\n\n## Farmers’ pain: climate, cash-flow and incentives\n> Arable farmers face repeated poor harvests, high input costs and removed payments, which together shrink cash flow and discourage planting.\n\nChair: Harry, what practical problem would make you stop growing food?\nHarry Metcalfe: Cash flow. I’d already spent 74% of 2027 costs. After successive bad years, I can’t invest. Taking away basic area payments exposed farmers to world prices and big weather risk.\nRupert Lowe: Taxes, net-zero costs and other levies make farming less competitive. You’re drilling crops at a loss hoping prices or weather improve—so should Government step back or act differently?\nJim Bligh: Long-term finance and partnerships help: retailers and suppliers can offer stability for big investments. But Government policy churn weakens that certainty.\nBalwinder Dhoot: Resilience needs investment. If businesses can’t make returns here, processing and production shift overseas and domestic capacity is lost.\n\n\n## Retail and processing: profit, energy and capacity\n> Retailers say they prioritise British supply but face 20% higher effective tax rates and big energy cost rises that reduce investment in UK processing.\n\nJim Bligh: Retailers do prioritise UK sourcing and long-term partnerships with farmers. But tax and energy increases—about £8bn extra since 2024—limit investment and resilience.\nChair: Is loss of UK processing—slaughterhouses, packhouses, roasting and canning—a threat?\nJim Bligh: Yes. Some processing remains here but key inputs (like CO2 or energy-intensive processing) are vulnerable. Where processing is cheaper abroad, the supply chain shifts overseas.\nBalwinder Dhoot: If processing leaves, factories won’t buy British ingredients. Energy competitiveness is central—if UK power costs are higher, investment goes elsewhere.\n\n\n## Critical infrastructure and local resilience\n> Witnesses urged mapping and protecting vital food assets, better local planning and more household preparedness to avoid panic-driven shortages.\n\nMr Betts: Should food supply chains be on the critical national infrastructure database?\nJim Bligh: Yes. Ports, large depots, food manufacturing hubs and cold chains are strategic assets and need clear identification and protection with the Cabinet Office and DEFRA working together.\nBalwinder Dhoot: You don’t need to capture every business. Identify key nodes—abattoirs, major processors, feed mills, fertiliser and CO2 supply—and test how resilient they are to fuel or grid problems.\nJim Bligh: Local resilience forums vary in capability. The best international examples link local planners directly with suppliers and test scenarios together; we should do more of that.\nHarry Metcalfe: Practical farming resilience—like reservoirs or water capture—is expensive and needs planning permission and grants; small farms struggle to afford those investments.\n\n\n## Government role: strategy, institutions and policy clarity\n> Experts say DEFRA lacks clout and continuity; they want a clear food policy, better national coordination and long-term signals to unlock private investment.\n\nRupert Lowe: Is DEFRA fit for purpose? Farmers need long-term plans, but frequent political change and policy churn create uncertainty for investment.\nProfessor Lang: We need a proper food policy, not just farming or environment policies. DEFRA is small, politically weak and often lacks the high-level pull to coordinate cross-Government action.\nProfessor Ward: Resilience is a public good. The state needs to convene industry, farmers and civil society, set long-term targets and fund transformations similar to post-war agricultural efforts.\nJim Bligh: Retailers can form long-term partnerships, but Government must prioritise food as critical infrastructure, consider energy support and be clearer on long-term strategy.\nProfessor Lang: We suggested renaming DEFRA to reflect food security. We need a national body or committee that can plan cross-departmentally—think Climate Change Committee but for food security.\n\n\n## Practical steps, international lessons and public preparedness\n> Suggested actions: map and protect key infrastructure, incentivise onshore processing and farming, devolve local powers, educate the public and include communities in planning.\n\nMatt Turmaine: Which countries do this well and what can we learn?\nProfessor Ward: Look at Finland, Sweden, the Netherlands, Japan and Israel. They take resilience seriously, plan institutionally and link national strategy to local capability.\nProfessor Lang: Examples vary: Sweden and Finland build public preparedness; France and parts of Belgium have strong regional food partnerships. Multi-level approaches matter.\nRachel Gilmour: How do we protect vulnerable people during disruption and how much is down to households?\nProfessor Lang: Households help, but community and local partnerships are key. Sweden plans multi-level storage and community support; Britain needs to build similar local capacity and include civil society in planning.\nChair: Final point from witnesses?\nHarry Metcalfe: My plea is simple: Government should understand farming’s seasonality and long-term nature, and give clearer signals and support so farmers can afford to grow food again.\nJim Bligh: Focus on long-term investment, protect key assets, and recognise food as critical national infrastructure so industry and Government can plan together.\nProfessor Ward: Treat resilience as a public good, convene a cross-Government effort and match land use to nutritious, resilient production over the next 25 years.\nProfessor Lang: Strengthen institutions, involve the public, protect supply diversity and unlock financing for resilient infrastructure. Small projects alone won’t be enough.\n",
